{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.SpechtAbsolutelyIrreducible","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.SpechtAbsolutelyIrreducible","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.SpechtIrreducible","lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.SpechtCrossKappa","lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.SpechtClassification"],"declarations":5,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.SpechtAbsolutelyIrreducible.simple_end_scalar","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.SpechtAbsolutelyIrreducible.simple_end_scalar","kind":"theorem","line":241,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/SpechtAbsolutelyIrreducible.lean#L241-L282","doc":"**No simple `k[S_n]`-module has a non-scalar commutant.** Over a characteristic-zero field, every\n`S_n`-equivariant `k`-linear endomorphism of a simple `k[S_n]`-module is a scalar.\n\nProof: transport an equivariant endomorphism along the classification isomorphism to the Specht\nmodel, apply `specht_end_scalar`, and transport back. The intertwiner is a `k`-linear equivalence, so\nthe scalar is preserved.\n\nThis is the module-theoretic form of the division-ring collapse: the commutant of a simple module is\nits Wedderburn block's division ring, and here it is the base field itself. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.SpechtAbsolutelyIrreducible.specht_end_comm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.SpechtAbsolutelyIrreducible.specht_end_comm","kind":"theorem","line":209,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/SpechtAbsolutelyIrreducible.lean#L209-L224","doc":"**The commutant is commutative.** An immediate consequence of `specht_end_scalar`: any two\nequivariant endomorphisms of `S^μ` commute, since both are scalars. This is the form in which the\nWedderburn block collapse consumes absolute irreducibility — a noncommutative division ring `𝔇 i`\nwould supply a noncommuting pair. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.SpechtAbsolutelyIrreducible.specht_end_scalar_unique","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.SpechtAbsolutelyIrreducible.specht_end_scalar_unique","kind":"theorem","line":194,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/SpechtAbsolutelyIrreducible.lean#L194-L207","doc":"The scalar in `specht_end_scalar` is **unique**: `S^μ` is nonzero (it contains a nonzero\npolytabloid), so two scalars agreeing on it are equal. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.SpechtAbsolutelyIrreducible.specht_end_scalar","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.SpechtAbsolutelyIrreducible.specht_end_scalar","kind":"theorem","line":141,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/SpechtAbsolutelyIrreducible.lean#L141-L192","doc":"**Absolute irreducibility of `S^μ` (the W2 content).** Over a characteristic-zero field, every\n`S_n`-equivariant `k`-linear endomorphism of the Specht module `S^μ` is a scalar.\n\nEquivalently: the commutant `End_{k[S_n]}(S^μ)` is `k` itself — no genuine division ring appears,\nwhich is precisely the collapse `𝔇 i ≃ k` that the abstract Wedderburn split\n(`WedderburnSn.sn_wedderburnArtin_alg`) leaves open over a non-algebraically-closed base.\n\n`-- Ref: [james1984representation] §4; [sagan2001] §2.4` ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.SpechtAbsolutelyIrreducible.endo_polytabloid_smul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.SpechtAbsolutelyIrreducible.endo_polytabloid_smul","kind":"theorem","line":69,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/SpechtAbsolutelyIrreducible.lean#L69-L139","doc":"**The commutant computation at one tableau.** An equivariant endomorphism of `S^μ` scales the\npolytabloid `e_t`.\n\nThis is the whole mathematical content: `κ_t` lands in the line `k·e_t` for *every* input\n(`kapL_mem_line`), while on `e_t` itself it acts by the nonzero scalar `|C_t|`\n(`kapL_polytabloid`). Equivariance moves `θ` past `κ_t`, and characteristic zero divides. ","signatureChunk":0}]}